<urn:atm:triage:finding/RSH-001>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "RSH-001" ;
  triage:title "new Task HTTP route silently drops the caller's RequestDeadline" ;
  triage:description "storage_and_nudge_router.rs:498-502: ApiRequest::Task(request) => CoreTaskCommandService::new(...).execute(*request).await never threads the ambient deadline: RequestDeadline parameter into execute(), unlike every sibling arm in the same dispatch_non_write match (list/peek/receive/clear/doctor/search/heartbeat/queue_get_next/graft_receiver_*). TaskCommandService::execute has no deadline parameter at all. CoreTaskCommandService instead uses a hardcoded TASK_COMMAND_READ_DEADLINE = Duration::from_secs(5) per read call inside mutate(), performing two such reads sequentially before an unbounded-at-this-layer write -- exceeding the product's canonical SERVER_REQUEST_BUDGET of 3s, while the client already computes and sends a real deadline for Task requests. This reintroduces the exact client/server request-budget mismatch class previously fixed in PR #1095, and regresses against sprint deliverable D2's own explicit requirement to preserve structured errors, deadlines, and retry operation ids. Confirmed present at head 6937c547f. Fix: thread the caller's RequestDeadline into CoreTaskCommandService::execute and bound its internal reads/mutation by the actual remaining budget instead of the hardcoded 5s constant. Source: QA-1 on PR #1382 (https://github.com/randlee/atm-core/pull/1382#issuecomment-5612459494)." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-az" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "runtime-hardening" ;
  triage:severity "blocking" ;
